e reported for either ticket", "State headlines center on the Senate race and 2028 primaries, not the presidential contest"], "rationale": "Montana's ten-point Republican lean stands as the anchor evidence, and nothing in the record — no polls, ratings, spending, or market signals — justifies moving from it. The Republican ticket is a clear but not overwhelming favorite, with uncertainty wide in the absence of polling.", "watch": "A late wave of credible state polling, an unusually strong or weak nominee, or a sharp shift in the national environment could move the margin several points in either direction.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "Montana remains one of the most Republican-leaning states on the presidential map, and the open-seat nature of the contest does little to change that. No public general-election polls, expert ratings, or prediction-market prices are available for this race, and neither side shows a reported fundraising edge. Headlines from the state focus on the Senate contest and the 2028 primary picture rather than the presidential race itself.\n\nWith the state's ten-point Republican lean as the anchor, the Republican ticket is expected to carry Montana by roughly ten points. Uncertainty is wide because polling is absent and the national environment could move either way. A Democratic win would require a substantial national swing well beyond anything currently indicated, making the Republican ticket a clear but not absolute favorite.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.1274, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.185, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.1774, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.1746, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_